{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>A healing of the tongue is a tree of life, and the twisting of it a breach in the spirit (Proverbs 15:4).</b>" "<b>King Solomon</b>, peace be upon him, informs us in this verse (Proverbs 15) of the magnitude of the benefit found in the tongue, for the tongue heals the illnesses of the soul, and its healing transcends all other healings. For the rest of the healings of drugs and medicaments or herbs — they are an uncertain healing, whether the patient will be healed by them or not, and even if he is healed by them, the entire power of their healing and their benefit extends only to the removal of the illness; there is in them no power to add to life. But the healing of the tongue is not an uncertain healing, rather it is a certain healing, and it has the power to add to a person's life — it is the tree of life, and life cleaves to it. And 'the twisting of it is a breach in the spirit': just as the healing of the tongue surpasses all other healings, so too the illness that comes from the tongue — and that is the disgrace, the mockery, and the derision by which a person shames his fellow and mocks and ridicules him, or whitens his face (publicly embarrasses him). And it is explicitly stated by our Rabbis of blessed memory: 'One who whitens the face of his fellow in public has no share in the World to Come.'" "<b>And behold,</b> this illness of the tongue is more severe than any illness of the body, and the most severe of all the illnesses of the body is 'a breach in the spirit,' that is, the breaking of the spirit. As it is written (Proverbs 18:14): ״רוח איש יכלכל מחלהו ורוח נכאה מי ישאנה״ — 'The spirit of a man sustains his infirmity, but a broken spirit who can bear?' The meaning of the verse is that the spirit — which is the soul — sustains the illness of the body, for it sustains the body's illness for a numbered number of days without food and drink; but when the soul is sick, who can bear it? And the verse comes to teach that the illness of the soul — which is the breaking of the spirit — is far more severe than the illness of the body. Furthermore, just as there is illness in the body, so too is there illness in the soul, and the illnesses of the soul are those who commit transgressions and one who holds a false faith — and this is what the verse mentions (Proverbs 2:16): ״להצילך מאשה זרה מנכריה אמריה החליקה״, and it is written (Proverbs 5:3): ״כי נופת תטופנה שפתי זרה״ — He compared false faith to a strange woman who destroys him in the end; therefore it says: 'and her latter end is bitter as wormwood, sharp as a two-edged sword,' and it is written: 'her feet go down to death, her steps lay hold of Sheol.' And by means of the faculty of speech of the tongue that is given to man, by which he has an advantage over all other living creatures, it is possible to heal one who has an illness of the soul and to draw him beneath the wings of the Shekhinah and to bequeath to him the true, eternal life. And this quality we found in Abraham our father, who strengthened and drew many to faith in HaShem, may He be blessed — and all this through the healing of the tongue. This is the pearl (margalit) that hung at his neck, as our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: 'There was a pearl hanging at the neck of Abraham our father; every sick person who saw it was immediately healed, and when he departed from the world, it was taken and hung in the sphere of the sun.' The explanation of this is that he compared this quality — which enlightens the eyes — to a luminous pearl, and said it was 'hanging at his neck' because speech is in the throat. And everyone who had an illness of the soul and heard the words of Abraham our father was immediately healed of his illness, and when he departed from the world he did not leave behind any created thing that testifies to the divinity of the Holy One, blessed be He, and to His unity, other than the sphere of the sun. Therefore it says 'it was taken and hung in the sphere of the sun,' in the manner of what is written (Psalms 19:2): ״השמים מספרים כבוד אל״. And this is what is said: 'A healing of the tongue is a tree of life' — the healing of the tongue of Abraham was the cause of many entering beneath the wings of the Shekhinah, and they acquired a tree of life. And as it is written (Genesis 12:5): ״ואת הנפש אשר עשו בחרן״, etc. The healing of the tongue of Moses our teacher, peace be upon him, when he told Jethro all those signs and wonders that are said, ״ויספר משה לחותנו״ — 'And Moses told his father-in-law' — was the cause of the conversion of Jethro, who was eminent and great in his family, and was the priest of Midian, and who left his wealth and most of his sons and separated from civilization and went out to the wilderness to enter beneath the wings of the Shekhinah as soon as he heard of the Exodus from Egypt. And this is what is written:" "<b>And Jethro, the priest of Midian, Moses' father-in-law, heard all that Elohim had done for Moses and for Israel His people, for YHWH had brought Israel out of Egypt.</b> In this parashah there are divergent opinions among our Rabbis of blessed memory: some say Jethro came before the giving of the Torah, and some say Jethro came after the giving of the Torah, and both these and those have proofs upon which to rely. Those who say he came after the giving of the Torah have support from verses in the parashah: for it says, 'unto the wilderness where he encamped there, the mountain of Elohim' — for it is well known that Moses and Israel encamped at Mount Sinai for one year, and this is what is written, ״אשר הוא חונה שם״, meaning that he was still encamped there at the mountain of Elohim, which indicates that he came after the giving of the Torah. Moreover, from what is written (Exodus 18): ״והודעתי את חקי האלהים ואת תורותיו״, it appears from this that the Torah had already been given. And furthermore, from what is written at the end of the parashah: ״וישלח משה את חותנו וילך לו אל ארצו״ — this too indicates that it was after the giving of the Torah, and therefore he sent him off and he returned to his land — for how could Jethro depart and not wait for the giving of the Torah? Now according to this view, one must wonder about what it says: 'And Jethro the priest of Midian, Moses' father-in-law, heard all that Elohim had done for Moses and for Israel His people, that YHWH had brought Israel out of Egypt' — and he did not mention the matter of the giving of the Torah, when it would have been fitting to say: that YHWH had brought Israel out of Egypt and that He made them hear His voice from within the fire. Moreover, regarding what it mentions, ״ויספר משה לחותנו את כל אשר עשה ה׳ לפרעה ולמצרים״, it would have been fitting for him to tell him in its entirety about the standing at Mount Sinai at the giving of the Torah; and if Jethro said regarding the signs and wonders that were done in Egypt, ״עתה ידעתי כי גדול ה׳ מכל האלהים״ — how much more so would he have said thus regarding the standing at Mount Sinai, for through it he could recognize and discern the truth of the principle of divinity by means of the great and awesome proofs that were there. By force of this reasoning, it appears that it is as those who say Jethro came before the giving of the Torah — and this view is more correct, and so too is the order of the parashiyot in their plain sense, that Jethro came before the giving of the Torah. This is the view of the Ramban, of blessed memory. And because of this, he did not mention the matter of the giving of the Torah within what he heard — for also what Moses our teacher, peace be upon him, recounted to him, ״את כל אשר עשה ה׳ לפרעה ולמצרים״, it was not yet possible for him to tell him about the giving of the Torah which was yet to come. And as for what it says, 'unto the wilderness where he encamped there, the mountain of Elohim' — it should not be understood that Moses was already encamped there at the mountain of Elohim; rather the matter is that Moses was at Rephidim, and Jethro set out from Midian with Zipporah and her sons and came to Rephidim, which is a certain place in the wilderness of Sin, between Elim and Sinai. And the verse relates that he came to the edge of the wilderness where he was. And what it says, 'the mountain of Elohim' — that mountain was already widely known to all, regarding the matter of that mountain, and on the understanding that they would serve Elohim on that mountain they had departed from Egypt. And the verse teaches you the merit of Jethro, that he forsook his land and his family to go from civilization into the wilderness, to that mountain, for the sake of the Name of the Holy One, blessed be He. And what it says, ״והודעתי את חקי האלהים ואת תורתיו״ — these are not the statutes of the Torah, which had not yet been given, but rather the statutes of the wilderness that Moses our teacher had already set for them, as it is written (Exodus 15:25): ״שם שם לו חק ומשפט״. And 'His Torahs' — these are the laws that had already been given to them at Marah: Sabbath and civil laws at Marah were given. As for what is written at the end of the parashah, ״וישלח משה את חותנו וילך לו אל ארצו״ — the intent is not that this was after the giving of the Torah and that Moses sent him and he returned to his land; for the verse did not say ״וישלח משה את חותנו וישב למקומו״, but rather said ״וילך לו אל ארצו״ — the intent is that he went there to convert his family members, and returned to Moses while he was still at Mount Sinai. And so did our Rabbis of blessed memory expound: Jethro said, 'I am going to convert the people of my city, to bring them beneath the wings of Heaven.' One might think he went and did not return — Scripture says (Judges 1:16): ״ובני קיני חותן משה עלו מעיר התמרים״. And the parashah of Jethro was juxtaposed to the parashah of Amalek, because the two were the opposite of each other: Amalek, the son of Esau, who is close to us, did evil with us, for he came from afar to fight against us in the wilderness; whereas Jethro, who was from the nations, did good with us, for he was like eyes for us on the way of the wilderness. And we were commanded to repay each and every one his fitting recompense: to blot out the memory of Amalek, as it is said (Deuteronomy 25:19): ״תמחה את זכר עמלק״, etc.; and to do good to Jethro, that we be careful with the children of the Kenite, who dwell among the Amalekites, not to destroy them when we destroy Amalek. And thus we find written (I Samuel 15:6): ״ויאמר שאול אל הקיני לכו סורו רדו מתוך עמלקי פן אוסיפך עמו ואתה עשית חסד עם כל בני ישראל בעלותם ממצרים ויסר קיני מתוך עמלק״." "<b>It is further possible</b> to give a reason for the juxtaposition of these parashiyot one to the other — the parashah of Jethro to the parashah of Amalek, and after it the parashah of 'In the third month,' where the giving of the Torah takes place, and after it the parashah of 'And these are the judgments.' It is well known that the seed of Esau is a thorn that pains Israel in all generations, both in the first war against Israel and in the last. And the verse alludes that just as we won the first war by means of Moses and Joshua, so too the last — which is this present exile, in whose hand we are — we are destined to be redeemed through Elijah, who is from the tribe of Levi like Moses, and Mashiach ben Yosef, who is from the tribe of Ephraim like Joshua. And just as in the first redemption, from Egypt, Jethro converted and returned to our faith, so too in the final redemption all the gentile nations will convert and return to our faith. Therefore the parashah of 'In the third month,' which is the giving of the Torah, was juxtaposed — for then the Torah will return to its lodging-place, and 'the earth shall be full of the knowledge of HaShem as the waters cover the sea.' And afterward will be the Day of Judgment of the resurrection of the dead. Therefore the parashah of 'And these are the judgments' was juxtaposed, for through the judgments and the laws, people go forth — these worthy and these liable. And so too the matter on the Day of Judgment of the resurrection of the dead, in which 'these' is written twice, as it is said (Daniel 12:2): ״אלה לחיי עולם ואלה לחרפות לדראון עולם״. If so, the juxtaposition of the parashiyot alludes and declares the end from the beginning, and behold, they are arranged with providence and with great intention." "<b>That YHWH brought Israel out.</b> After it mentioned the expression of Elohim's action, it wished to mention the special Name in connection with the bringing out, to make clear that the special Name is the One who brings out — as in the manner of what is written (Exodus 13:16): ״כי בחזק יד הוציאנו ה' ממצרים״ — for He is the One who brings out with a strong hand, not 'strength of hand' alone. And understand this, for I have already mentioned it."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:01:15.279418489Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}